      "thinking": "I notice the real fault: `record_a_purchase` writes to the ledger but nothing then invalidates or refreshes the cached summary entry, so cached copies drift out of sync. Though I also wonder if there's a subtler shape mismatch issue, where older writers stored a bare figure instead of the amount/count structure the cache now expects.\n\nLooking deeper, I realize there's no write path to the cache at all in this repo, so \"the write path broke\" isn't the right framing. The actual issue is on the read side: `account_page._the_figure_for` returns `found.amount_cents` directly from the cache without verifying it's current or matches the right rollout variant \u2014 so a figure computed under one cohort gets served to a different one, which is a correctness problem but distinct from the staleness issue I'm trying to pin down.\n\nThe real fix available in the code is that each cache entry carries `items_counted`, which can be compared against `len(account.purchases)` cheaply \u2014 if they disagree, the entry is stale and must be recomputed rather than served. The cache_reconciliation module's docstring even distinguishes \"stale\" from \"miscomputed\" using this exact field, confirming that the read path should treat a mismatched `items_counted` as a cache miss rather than blindly trusting whatever's stored, which is currently why a frozen figure gets served forever once replication lags.\n\nThis makes the cache self-correcting: a stale copy gets discarded on read, the page recomputes from the ledger, and I'd mark `served_from_cache` false since it was recomputed. I also want to avoid silencing the staleness entirely, so I'm considering adding a `stale_cache` field to RenderedPage (distinct from `cache_failure`, which means unreachable) to record that a stale entry was passed over \u2014 a modest, backward-compatible addition. Now I should check other callers of `serve_account_page` and `RenderedPage` to make sure this doesn't break anything.\n\n",
